==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|loitren|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{term|loteren||to shake, wag, wobble|lang=dum}}, ultimately connected with a frequentative form of {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*lūtaną||to bend, stoop, cower, shrink from, decline}}, see {{l|en|lout}}. Cognate with Modern Dutch {{term|leuteren||to dawdle|lang=nl}}, Swiss German {{term|lottern||to wobble|lang=gsw}}, German {{term|Lotterbube||rascal|lang=de}}. More at {{l|en|lout}}, {{l|en|little}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈlɔɪtə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈlɔɪtɚ/|[ˈlɔjɾɚ]|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-loiter.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔɪtə(r)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To stand about without any aim or purpose; to stand about [[idly]]; to [[linger]]; to [[hang around]].
#: ''For some reason, they discourage '''loitering''' outside the store, but encourage it inside.''
#* {{quote-news|author=Daniel Taylor|title=David Silva seizes point for Manchester City as Chelsea are checked|work={{w|The Guardian}} (London)|date=31 January 2015|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jan/31/chelsea-manchester-city-premier-league-match-report
|passage=Agüero, as usual, was '''loitering''' with intent and swung his left foot at the ball. The shot was going wide but Silva was there to apply the decisive touch inside the six-yard area.}}

